@title AWS landing zone
@subtitle Meridian Logistics · Cloud Foundations team · as of 2026-10-05

# Account structure

diagram lz dir=LR title="AWS landing zone"
lz: org "AWS Organizations" svc
lz: idc "IAM Identity Center" svc
lz: gd "GuardDuty" svc
lz: sh "Security Hub" svc
lz: logb "Log Archive Bucket" store
lz: tgw "Transit Gateway" svc
lz: nat "NAT Gateway" svc
lz: prodapp "Prod App Subnets" subnet
lz: proddata "Prod Data Subnets" subnet
lz: devapp "Dev App Subnets" subnet
lz: prodvpc -> tgw : attachment
lz: devvpc -> tgw : attachment
lz: tgw -> nat : 0.0.0.0/0
lz: gd -> sh : findings
lz: org -> logb : CloudTrail logs
lz: group egress "Egress VPC" nat
lz: group network "Network account" tgw, egress
lz: group prodvpc "Prod VPC" prodapp, proddata
lz: group prod "Prod workload account" prodvpc
lz: group devvpc "Dev VPC" devapp
lz: group dev "Dev workload account" devvpc
lz: group mgmt "Management account" org, idc
lz: group sec "Security account" gd, sh
lz: group logs "Log Archive account" logb

# Network ranges

table = VPC,Account,CIDR,Region; Egress VPC,Network,10.0.0.0/20,us-west-2; Prod VPC,Prod workload,10.10.0.0/16,us-west-2; Dev VPC,Dev workload,10.20.0.0/16,us-west-2

# Guardrails

Service control policies in force across the organization:

- Deny leaving the organization.
- Deny disabling CloudTrail.
- Deny regions other than us-west-2 and us-east-1.
- Deny root user access keys.

! Prod and Dev VPCs cannot route to each other. The Transit Gateway uses separate route tables, rt-prod and rt-dev.
